The scripts/api/ auth and CRUD layer, doctor.sh and validate-spec.py
originate there, with local modifications: a new scripts/load-env.sh, a
separate state directory so this kit's refresh token does not collide with
the upstream skill's, trimmed CI, and validate-spec.py reduced to the checks
this kit can actually fire. Other inherited tooling (sigma-resolve.py,
workbook-manifest.py, harvest-workbook.sh, the Cowork/Cortex skill-packaging
harness, and 12 of validate-spec.py's checks) has since been removed as
unused here. One of those checks, `action-refs-resolve`, was restored
2026-09-15 -- the removal had left the validator's closing note advertising
it, so every run since claimed a referential-integrity pass it was not making.
Eleven remain removed.

Some of that code is in turn adapted from Sigma's own reference skill pair,
distributed as dated zip releases rather than a repository:

    sigma-api / sigma-workbooks  (Sigma Computing)
    delivered as sigma-workbook-skills-<YYYY-MM-DD>.zip

Ported from it: scripts/api/verify-workbook.sh (near-verbatim, rewired to
_env.sh + sigma_curl), scripts/api/browser-login.sh (the OAuth
authorization-code + PKCE flow, then extended here with the headless
--start/--finish phases that Claude Cowork's sandboxed bash needs and upstream
has no equivalent of), and several of validate-spec.py's checks plus its YAML
support.

UPSTREAM SYNC STATE. Because releases arrive as same-named dated zips, which
one a port came from is easy to lose -- record it here on every sync.

  * Ports made on or before 2026-08-03: release not recorded. Their in-file
    notes predate the 2026-08-07 zip, so they came either from an earlier
    release or via ryan-workbook-skill, which vendors the same code.
  * 2026-09-15: reviewed against the 2026-08-07 release. Cherry-picked two
    facts into docs/elements-known-good.md (pluginId is unvalidated on write;
    the canonical and plural column-binding forms). Nothing else adopted --
    this kit's own findings on the document envelope, flat document.elements,
    grouped-element binding and asset versioning are ahead of it. That
    release is now installed separately as a personal skill for authoring
    ordinary workbooks; it is deliberately NOT vendored here, since this kit
    generates only the one page that hosts a plugin.
  * A 2026-06-11 zip also exists. It is stale and WRONG on the spec's most
    load-bearing point -- it teaches elements nested under pages[] and no
    document envelope, both of which the API now rejects outright. Do not
    port from it.

Plugin conventions -- the synthetic-fallback idea and POST /v2/plugins
registration -- were derived from:

    millersigma
    https://github.com/cmiller-coder/millersigma
    Copyright (c) Connor Miller

    whose plugin skills in turn credit neil-oliver/sigma-plugin-skills.

Two further conventions taken from there have since been dropped: the
single-file index.html pattern and loading the SDK from a CDN. Every plugin is
now a Vite + React project that bundles @sigmacomputing/plugin from npm, and
deploy-plugin.sh refuses anything without a package.json. The reason is in
docs/plugins.md -> "Why there is no hand-written-HTML archetype": a page that
pulls the UMD bundle from a CDN without loading React first leaves
window.SigmaPlugin a bare {}, and the plugin renders its fallback forever.

No code was copied from millersigma; plugins/_react-template/ is written from
scratch.

